// gfxdemo -- the first windowed program in `id`. It opens a 320x200 window via
// the native graphics backend (host/dev/win/gfx), then runs a frame loop that draws
// a moving box into a software framebuffer and presents it. The drawing is all
// portable `id`; only gfx_open/present/poll/close cross into platform code.
//
//   idc/bin/idc demos/gfxdemo --allow-untested -o gfxdemo && ./gfxdemo
//
// Close the window (or press Esc / q) to quit. Like the terminal engine, every
// function obeys id's rules -- 3 actions, 2-deep nesting, 3 functions per file.

// main()'s no-display path: the build harness runs every case with IDSTD_GFX=none (docs/TESTS.md), which makes
// gfx_open() fail the same way on every machine, whatever its own DISPLAY
// is, so ok=0 and loop(ok) returns through spin's run<=0 branch without
// entering the live poll -- the one path that does not depend on
// GFX_MAX_FRAMES, an environment variable this build does not export. The
// GFX_MAX_FRAMES-bounded present/poll cycle still cannot be cased this way:
// that needs a real, open display, which is what tools/headless.sh's Xvfb
// (tests/backends.sh) covers separately.
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  int ok = gfx_open(320, 200, "hey look ma i made it");
  sf_l_init(320, 200);
  loop(ok);
} return int 0;
(0, []):(0) then idstd_sf_l_t_sum:("px 0")
(0, []):(0) then calls sf_l_init:(320, 200)

// anim/box/box_paint draw a 40x30 box, so a surface smaller than that would
// turn their x0/y0 modulo arithmetic against a non-positive divisor; 80x60
// is the smallest round size that keeps it positive and leaves gfx_big_w a
// clean value to check.
gfx_big_setup() {
  sf_l_init(80, 60);
} return void;
():() then calls sf_l_init:(80, 60)
():() then idstd_sf_l_t_sum:("px 0")

